Subject: ntdll: Report a 41-bit user address space to Chromium child processes on Android.

Android kernels give 64-bit processes a 39-bit (512 GB) address space. V8 with the V8 sandbox
(Chrome / CEF 128 and newer) refuses to start unless GetSystemInfo reports more than its 1 TB
sandbox: CHECK_LT(kSandboxSize, address_space_limit) in Sandbox::Initialize -> int3. Seen with the
EA app 13.836 (EA's own CEF 128.4.13, Chrome 128.0.6613.138, forced update 2026-11-26): every
EACefSubProcess renderer died at libcef+0x9f68cc, the sign-in page never drew. Past the check V8
uses a partially reserved sandbox that fits in 512 GB (proven on the Pocket FIT by skipping the
check in libcef: page loads, HTTP 200, no crash).

Only the value reported through NtQuerySystemInformation (basic / native / emulation basic info)
changes, and only for processes with "--type=" on the command line (Chromium/CEF/Electron
children); Wine keeps using the real limit internally, and MEM_ADDRESS_REQUIREMENTS upper limits
up to the reported value are clamped to the real one. WINE_LARGE_ADDRESS_REPORT=1 reports it to
every 64-bit process, =0 to none. Kernels with >= 41-bit address spaces are unaffected.

